    "learningObjective": "Analyze the sequence of European trading companies in India, the strategic outcomes of Plassey and Buxar, and the constitutional impact of the 1765 Treaty of Allahabad.",
    "introduction": "The arrival of European maritime powers began with Vasco da Gama landing at Calicut in 1498 (Portuguese), followed by the Dutch East India Company (1602), English East India Company (1600), and the French Compagnie des Indes Orientales (1664). The British victory under Robert Clive at Plassey (23 June 1757) against Siraj-ud-Daulah, followed by the decisive Battle of Buxar (22 October 1764) under Hector Munro, transformed a merchant corporation into the territorial ruler of Bengal, Bihar, and Orissa.",
    "detailedExplanation": "<h3>1. Sequence of European Advent in India</h3>\n<p>The sequence of arrival of European commercial enterprises was: <strong>Portuguese (1498) &rarr; Dutch (1602) &rarr; English (1600 charter, factory at Surat 1613) &rarr; Danish (1616) &rarr; French (1664)</strong>. The Anglo-French rivalry in the Carnatic (1746\u20131763) was settled by the Battle of Wandiwash (1760), where Sir Eyre Coote crushed the French under Count de Lally.</p>\n<h3>2. The Battle of Plassey (23 June 1757)</h3>\n<p>Triggered by the misuse of <em>dastaks</em> (duty-free trade permits) and the fortification of Calcutta. Robert Clive entered into a secret conspiracy with Mir Jafar (commander-in-chief), Rai Durlabh, and Jagat Seth (banker). Siraj-ud-Daulah was betrayed and defeated. Plassey established British political paramountcy in Bengal and installed Mir Jafar as the 'puppet Nawab'.</p>\n<h3>3. Battle of Buxar (22 October 1764) & Treaty of Allahabad (1765)</h3>\n<p>Unlike Plassey (a skirmish decided by treachery), Buxar was a pitched military contest. <strong>Major Hector Munro</strong> routed the combined confederacy of <strong>Mir Qasim</strong> (ex-Nawab of Bengal), <strong>Shuja-ud-Daulah</strong> (Nawab of Awadh), and Mughal Emperor <strong>Shah Alam II</strong>. This established British military superiority over all of Northern India.</p>\n<p>Under the historic <strong>Treaty of Allahabad (16 August 1765)</strong> executed by Robert Clive:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Mughal Emperor Shah Alam II granted the <strong>Diwani</strong> (revenue collection rights) of Bengal, Bihar, and Orissa to the English East India Company in return for an annual pension of Rs. 26 lakhs.</li>\n  <li>Districts of Kora and Allahabad were ceded to the Emperor from Awadh.</li>\n  <li>Shuja-ud-Daulah paid an indemnity of Rs. 50 lakhs and Awadh became a British buffer state.</li>\n  <li>Clive instituted the <strong>Dual Government of Bengal (1765\u20131772)</strong>: Diwani (fiscal revenue) with Company, Nizamat (police and civil administration) with the puppet Nawab without funds. Warren Hastings abolished it in 1772.</li>\n</ul>",
