    "title": "Integration of Princely States (Sardar Patel & V.P. Menon), Linguistic Reorganization & Republic Formation",
    "subtitle": "Instrument of Accession, Junagadh, Hyderabad (Op Polo), Kashmir, States Reorganization Act 1956 & 26 Jan 1950",
    "learningObjective": "Master the diplomatic integration of 565 princely states under Sardar Patel, resolution of recalcitrant states, linguistic reorganization, and Republic inauguration.",
    "introduction": "Upon the lapse of British paramountcy on 15 August 1947, India faced the existential threat of Balkanization across 565 autonomous princely states. Through resolute diplomacy, patriotic appeal, and strategic firmness, Deputy Prime Minister Sardar Vallabhbhai Patel ('Iron Man of India') and Secretary V.P. Menon integrated almost all states via the Instrument of Accession. The subsequent integration of Junagadh, Hyderabad, and Jammu & Kashmir, followed by the Fazal Ali States Reorganization Commission and the adoption of the Constitution on 26 January 1950, welded India into a sovereign democratic republic.",
    "detailedExplanation": "<h3>1. Integration of 565 Princely States: Patel and V.P. Menon</h3>\n<p>The Ministry of States was created on 27 June 1947 headed by <strong>Sardar Vallabhbhai Patel</strong> with <strong>V.P. Menon</strong> as Secretary. Patel appealed to princes' patriotism, offering them privy purses and retention of personal titles while requesting surrender of only three subjects: <strong>Defense, External Affairs, and Communications</strong> via the <strong>Instrument of Accession</strong>.</p>\n<p>By 15 August 1947, all states except three contiguous to India had signed:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Junagadh:</strong> Nawab Muhammad Mahabat Khan III announced accession to Pakistan despite 80% Hindu population. The population revolted, forming an <em>Arzi Hukumat</em> (Provisional Government) under Samaldas Gandhi. The Nawab fled to Karachi. Following a plebiscite in February 1948 (voting 99% for India), Junagadh merged with India.</li>\n  <li><strong>Hyderabad:</strong> Nizam Mir Osman Ali Khan refused accession, desiring an independent sovereign state. His armed irregular militia, the <strong>Razakars</strong> (led by Kasim Razvi), unleashed widespread terror on the civilian population. In September 1948, the Indian Army launched <strong>Operation Polo</strong> (police action) under Major-General J.N. Chaudhuri. Hyderabad surrendered on 17 September 1948 and acceded to the Indian Union.</li>\n  <li><strong>Jammu & Kashmir:</strong> Maharaja Hari Singh signed a Standstill Agreement. On 22 October 1947, Pakistani armed forces and tribal Pathans invaded Kashmir. On <strong>26 October 1947</strong>, Maharaja Hari Singh signed the <strong>Instrument of Accession</strong> with India. Indian airborne troops landed at Srinagar on 27 October, repelling the invaders.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>2. Linguistic Reorganization of States</h3>\n<p>The demand for linguistic reorganization emerged vigorously post-independence:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>SK Dhar Commission (June 1948):</strong> Recommended reorganization based on administrative convenience rather than linguistic basis.</li>\n  <li><strong>JVP Committee (December 1948):</strong> Comprising Jawaharlal Nehru, Vallabhbhai Patel, and Pattabhi Sitaramayya; also rejected linguistic basis for the time being.</li>\n  <li><strong>Creation of Andhra State (1953):</strong> Following a 56-day hunger strike, Gandhian leader <strong>Potti Sreeramulu</strong> died on 15 December 1952. Widespread unrest forced the government to create <strong>Andhra State</strong> (Telugu-speaking from Madras) on <strong>1 October 1953</strong> as the <strong>first linguistic state</strong> of India.</li>\n  <li><strong>Fazal Ali Commission (States Reorganization Commission, 1953):</strong> Members: <strong>Justice Fazal Ali (Chairman), H.N. Kunzru, and K.M. Panikkar</strong>. Accepted language as the basis of reorganization while rejecting 'one language, one state'. Enacted as the <strong>States Reorganization Act 1956</strong> (along with 7th Constitutional Amendment), creating <strong>14 States and 6 Union Territories</strong> on 1 November 1956.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>3. Republic Inauguration (26 January 1950)</h3>\n<p>The Constituent Assembly adopted the Constitution on 26 November 1949. It came into full effect on <strong>26 January 1950</strong> (celebrated as Republic Day), commemorating the historic 1930 Purna Swaraj pledge day. Dr. Rajendra Prasad was sworn in as the first President of India, completing the transition from a British Dominion to a Sovereign Democratic Republic.</p>",
